WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.071
Here's a question we rarely ask out loud.

2
00:00:05.071 --> 00:00:09.352
When the first step of a chain throws, what happens to the steps after it?

3
00:00:09.352 --> 00:00:12.128
Do they run anyway, with nothing in their hands?

4
00:00:12.833 --> 00:00:14.134
Back to the kitchen.

5
00:00:14.134 --> 00:00:21.938
Last time, the ticket for table 4 carried its own instructions: grill the steak, pour the sauce, plate it, carry it out.

6
00:00:21.938 --> 00:00:26.165
The ticket was a CompletableFuture, and each instruction a stage.

7
00:00:26.367 --> 00:00:27.633
Now the steak burns.

8
00:00:27.633 --> 00:00:31.241
The sauce cook has no business pouring sauce on charcoal.

9
00:00:31.241 --> 00:00:34.407
So a note goes on the ticket: burned at the grill.

10
00:00:34.407 --> 00:00:40.991
Every later station passes it along, untouched, to the pass, the counter where dishes leave the kitchen.

11
00:00:40.991 --> 00:00:44.789
Someone there reads the note, and decides what table 4 gets.

12
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:45.921
Let's map it.

13
00:00:45.921 --> 00:00:48.757
Each station is a stage, like thenApply.

14
00:00:48.757 --> 00:00:50.600
The note is the exception.

15
00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:56.483
The pass is a handler, a stage that also runs after a failure: here, exceptionally.

16
00:00:56.700 --> 00:00:59.503
The lesson's ErrorPathDemo builds this line.

17
00:00:59.503 --> 00:01:02.178
The grill always throws: the steak burned.

18
00:01:02.178 --> 00:01:04.854
Each numbered station prints what it does.

19
00:01:04.854 --> 00:01:10.715
Then whenComplete, the manager, glances at every ticket going past, and the pass comes last.

20
00:01:10.715 --> 00:01:12.435
Which numbered lines print?

21
00:01:15.233 --> 00:01:15.588
None.

22
00:01:15.588 --> 00:01:17.079
Their code never ran.

23
00:01:17.079 --> 00:01:20.274
The manager saw the failure and passed it on.

24
00:01:20.274 --> 00:01:25.244
The pass caught it, and returned: table 4 got an apology and the menu.

25
00:01:25.467 --> 00:01:27.177
Not the dinner they ordered.

26
00:01:27.177 --> 00:01:29.621
But it beats charcoal with pepper sauce.

27
00:01:29.621 --> 00:01:34.020
So the point is, a failure skips every stage until it reaches a handler.

28
00:01:34.233 --> 00:01:37.699
Where you put the handler decides what you can still save.

29
00:01:37.699 --> 00:01:42.120
Move the pass up, right after the grill, and it can swap in a spare steak.

30
00:01:42.120 --> 00:01:45.645
Then every station after it works as if nothing went wrong.

31
00:01:45.867 --> 00:01:48.753
So recover as close to the failure as you can.

32
00:01:49.567 --> 00:01:52.439
Now look at what the pass actually received.

33
00:01:52.439 --> 00:01:54.398
Not our IllegalStateException.

34
00:01:54.398 --> 00:01:57.010
A CompletionException, with ours inside.

35
00:01:57.010 --> 00:01:59.556
Our note arrived sealed in an envelope.

36
00:01:59.767 --> 00:02:04.807
So if the pass asks, is this an IllegalStateException, the answer is no.

37
00:02:04.807 --> 00:02:05.997
It's an envelope.

38
00:02:05.997 --> 00:02:07.957
That check is quietly wrong.

39
00:02:08.767 --> 00:02:13.257
The rule: an exception thrown inside a stage travels in an envelope.

40
00:02:13.257 --> 00:02:21.048
A future completed by hand, with completeExceptionally or a timer, holds it bare, until the next stage seals it again.

41
00:02:21.048 --> 00:02:24.680
So open the envelope with getCause, then test the type.

42
00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:26.481
Now that timer.

43
00:02:26.481 --> 00:02:34.130
The restaurant buys flour, and each supplier has a web service, a program on another computer that answers questions.

44
00:02:34.130 --> 00:02:39.360
Asking for a price is a blocking call: it holds a thread until the answer comes.

45
00:02:39.360 --> 00:02:40.929
So, a cook on the phone.

46
00:02:41.133 --> 00:02:43.318
The manager sets a kitchen timer.

47
00:02:43.318 --> 00:02:45.040
In code, that's orTimeout.

48
00:02:45.040 --> 00:02:50.470
When it rings, the future fails with a TimeoutException, and the manager moves on.

49
00:02:50.470 --> 00:02:52.125
What happens to the cook?

50
00:02:54.900 --> 00:03:01.310
The lesson's KeepsRunningDemo gives a 600 millisecond call a deadline of 200.

51
00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:05.639
The future fails right on time, at 236 milliseconds.

52
00:03:05.639 --> 00:03:06.721
And the cook?

53
00:03:06.721 --> 00:03:07.887
Still on hold.

54
00:03:07.887 --> 00:03:12.299
The supplier answers at 695, and nobody is listening.

55
00:03:12.500 --> 00:03:17.732
He's still listening to the supplier's hold music, the same four notes, over and over.

56
00:03:17.732 --> 00:03:23.329
So the timer freed the manager, not the cook, and the thread stays busy until the call ends.

57
00:03:23.533 --> 00:03:24.627
And cancel(true)?

58
00:03:24.627 --> 00:03:27.459
It returns true, and the call never notices.

59
00:03:27.459 --> 00:03:29.004
Here the picture breaks.

60
00:03:29.004 --> 00:03:32.865
A real manager could walk over and tell the cook to hang up.

61
00:03:32.865 --> 00:03:37.241
A CompletableFuture keeps no line back to the thread doing the work.

62
00:03:37.467 --> 00:03:40.623
So a call to another computer needs two timeouts.

63
00:03:40.623 --> 00:03:43.136
The one on the future frees the caller.

64
00:03:43.136 --> 00:03:50.287
The one inside the client, such as a timeout on the HTTP request, makes the call give up, and frees the thread.

65
00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:56.831
Now the manager wants three prices at once, from the corner shop, the market and the mill.

66
00:03:56.831 --> 00:03:58.520
Three cooks phone together.

67
00:03:58.520 --> 00:04:03.337
Sending several calls at once and collecting the answers is called a fan-out.

68
00:04:03.567 --> 00:04:10.139
In FanOutDemo, the corner shop fails after 50 milliseconds, because it's closed today.

69
00:04:10.139 --> 00:04:15.719
The market answers 21.50 after 100, and the mill answers 19.80 after 300.

70
00:04:15.719 --> 00:04:17.706
allOf waits for all three.

71
00:04:17.706 --> 00:04:19.999
What does it hand the manager?

72
00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:29.949
A failure, at 336 milliseconds.

73
00:04:29.949 --> 00:04:33.830
Two good prices came back, and the errand failed anyway.

74
00:04:33.830 --> 00:04:37.989
If any future fails, allOf fails, after the slowest is done.

75
00:04:37.989 --> 00:04:40.970
And anyOf, which takes the first to finish?

76
00:04:40.970 --> 00:04:43.535
The first news here was the bad news.

77
00:04:43.767 --> 00:04:47.941
The fix: make each call unable to fail before you combine them.

78
00:04:47.941 --> 00:04:54.831
Each call gets its own deadline, then a handle, which turns a failure into no answer, an empty Optional.

79
00:04:55.033 --> 00:04:55.973
Run it again.

80
00:04:55.973 --> 00:05:04.504
The corner shop becomes no answer, and at 353 milliseconds the next step picks the lowest price: 19.80, from the mill.

81
00:05:04.504 --> 00:05:08.118
The cheapest flour came from the slowest supplier.

82
00:05:08.118 --> 00:05:10.143
Good thing we waited for it.

83
00:05:11.067 --> 00:05:13.599
Here's one safe call in Java and Kotlin.

84
00:05:13.599 --> 00:05:15.561
Keep the deadline on each call.

85
00:05:15.561 --> 00:05:20.815
One deadline on allOf would let a single hung supplier throw away the good answers.

86
00:05:21.633 --> 00:05:24.031
So, do the steps after a failure run?

87
00:05:24.031 --> 00:05:24.226
No.

88
00:05:24.226 --> 00:05:29.216
The note rides the ticket to the first handler, so recover close to the burn.

89
00:05:29.216 --> 00:05:32.002
Open the envelope before you read the note.

90
00:05:32.002 --> 00:05:35.113
A kitchen timer frees the manager, not the cook.

91
00:05:35.113 --> 00:05:38.548
And make each call unable to fail before you combine.

92
00:05:39.267 --> 00:05:40.156
Quick check.

93
00:05:40.156 --> 00:05:41.342
The grill fails.

94
00:05:41.342 --> 00:05:45.790
Then come thenApply A, then exceptionally, then thenApply B.

95
00:05:45.790 --> 00:05:47.421
Which of A and B runs?

96
00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:50.909
Only B.

97
00:05:50.909 --> 00:05:54.403
A is skipped, and exceptionally hands B a value.
